## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design a scalable streaming PCA system across heterogeneous cameras while handling real-world challenges like missing data, feature drift, and strict latency constraints.

## Key Concepts
- **Online Incremental PCA**: Efficiently updating principal components without full recomputation
- **Per-camera Normalization**: Z-score normalization to account for varying lighting conditions across cameras
- **Missing Frame Handling**: Forward-fill imputation using last-valid frame to maintain temporal consistency
- **Cross-camera Drift Detection**: Monitoring loading divergence via cosine similarity to detect when basis recalibration is needed
- **Latency Budgeting**: Balancing update frequency against real-time dashboard requirements

## Evaluation Plan
1. **Reconstruction Error**: Track MSE on held-out validation set to verify 16 components retain sufficient variance
2. **Drift Detection**: Measure cosine similarity of loadings over time; trigger recalibration when threshold breached
3. **Latency**: Profile end-to-end pipeline (ingestion → normalization → PCA → dashboard) targeting <100ms p99
4. **Ablation**: Compare forward-fill vs. zero-imputation vs. interpolation for missing frames

## Concrete Retention Rationale
With 1024 dimensions, 16 components captures ~85-95% variance for typical video features (empirical). The 500-frame window (~16 seconds at 30fps) balances capturing stable backgrounds while excluding stale data. This provides a practical trade-off between representation quality and adaptation speed.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Streaming PCA update with drift detection
function updatePCA(state, frame) {
  const x = perCameraNormalize(frame, state.cameraStats);
  const imputed = imputeMissing(x, state.lastValid);

  const newState = incrementalUpdate(state, imputed);

  if (cosineSimilarity(state.loadings, newState.loadings) < DRIFT_THRESHOLD) {
    return { ...newState, recalibrate: true };
  }

  return newState;
}
```

